tasks.prompt:
Build the Members page of an admin console for a workspace of AI agents. Members are people in the workspace. The page lists them, each with their name, email, role and team; an admin is marked as one. Search narrows the list by name or email. On a wide screen, the member chosen in the list shows in a pane beside it, and moving through the list with the arrow keys moves the pane with it; below 768px wide, choosing a member opens their details over the page instead. The details are their facts (email with a way to copy it, team, role, joined, last active, or 'invited' if they have never signed in) and the agents they own, each with its status. The agents come from a second call that can fail on its own: the pane then says so, while the list and the facts stay. Loading, empty (no members yet, and no matches for a search) and error states are all required.

The data layer exists. Copy these declarations into your file exactly as they are, and do not implement or import them:

type IsoDay = string; // YYYY-MM-DD
interface Member { id: string; name: string; initials: string; email: string; role: 'admin' | 'member'; team: string; joined: IsoDay; lastActiveMinutesAgo: number | null }
interface MemberAgent { id: string; name: string; status: 'running' | 'paused' | 'failing' | 'draft' }
declare function listMembers(signal: AbortSignal): Promise<Member[]>;
declare function getMemberAgents(memberId: string, signal: AbortSignal): Promise<MemberAgent[]>;

Build the Agents page of an admin console for a workspace of AI agents. It is a table of agents: name with its purpose, status, owning team, owner, runs in the last 7 days, success rate and last run. Columns sort, and the sort applies to every match, not one page. The table pages, with a choice of page size. Above it: search, a status filter and a multi-choice team filter, with the active filters shown and removable one by one. Rows can be selected, including every row matching the filters across all pages; with a selection, bulk actions appear to pause, resume or delete the selected agents. Each row also has its own actions menu. Deleting asks first and names what will be deleted; it can partly fail, and then says which agents were not deleted and why. A refilter keeps the current rows on screen while the next ones load. Required states: loading, empty (no agents exist, which is different from none matching the filters), error, and partial: a row whose metrics failed shows that instead of a number.

The data layer exists. Copy these declarations into your file exactly as they are, and do not implement or import them:

type IsoDay = string;
type AgentStatus = 'running' | 'paused' | 'failing' | 'draft';
interface Agent { id: string; name: string; purpose: string; status: AgentStatus; team: string; owner: { name: string; initials: string }; runs7d: number | null; successRate: number | null; lastRun: IsoDay | null }
interface AgentQuery { search: string; status: AgentStatus | 'all'; teams: readonly string[]; sort: { column: 'name' | 'runs7d' | 'successRate' | 'lastRun'; direction: 'ascending' | 'descending' }; page: number; pageSize: number }
interface AgentPage { rows: Agent[]; total: number; workspaceTotal: number; degraded: number }
declare const TEAMS: { value: string; label: string }[];
declare function listAgents(query: AgentQuery, signal: AbortSignal): Promise<AgentPage>;
declare function setPaused(ids: string[], paused: boolean): Promise<void>;
declare function deleteAgents(ids: string[]): Promise<{ deleted: string[]; failed: { id: string; name: string; reason: string }[] }>;

Build the New agent flow of an admin console for a workspace of AI agents: a multi-step form reached from the Agents page, with a way back to it. Step 1, Basics: name (required), purpose (required, longer text), owning team and model side by side on a wide screen. Step 2, Trigger: how the agent starts, chosen from three options each with a sentence (on a schedule, on an incoming email, or manually). On a schedule, it asks for the first run as a date and time that cannot be in the past, and an optional end date that cannot be before the first run. Step 3, Knowledge: optional files to upload, refusing types other than PDF, CSV and TXT, and files over 10 MB, with the reason shown on the field; then which tools the agent may use, at least one. Step 4, Review: everything entered, with a way to edit each part. Each step validates before moving on and shows a summary of what is missing, with links to each field. The last step creates the agent, or creates and starts it. The steps show progress, and going back keeps what was entered.

The data layer exists. Copy these declarations into your file exactly as they are, and do not implement or import them:

interface NewAgent { name: string; purpose: string; team: string; model: string; trigger: 'schedule' | 'email' | 'manual'; firstRun?: string; endsOn?: string; files: File[]; tools: string[] }
declare const TEAMS: { value: string; label: string }[];
declare const MODELS: { value: string; label: string }[];
declare const TOOLS: { value: string; label: string; description: string }[];
declare function createAgent(agent: NewAgent, start: boolean): Promise<{ id: string }>;

Build the Agent runs page of an admin console for a workspace of AI agents. One run is open at a time. It streams its steps as the agent works, each step a plain-language line; a step that called a tool can show that call's input and output. Some steps stop for a person's approval: the proposal shows its risk and its lines, with approve and reject, and edit where the proposal can be amended. A run can be stopped while it works. When it ends, the page says how: completed with its result, failed and at which step, stopped, or rejected. Beside the open run: a queue of runs waiting for someone's approval, and a history of recent runs (task, agent, outcome, started, duration, reviewers) filterable by how long they took, each opening its details beside the history.

The data layer exists. Copy these declarations into your file exactly as they are, and do not implement or import them:

type Risk = 'low' | 'medium' | 'high';
interface WorkStep { kind: 'work'; active: string; done: string; detail: string; failure?: string; ms: number; tool?: { title: string; name: string; input: Record<string, unknown>; output: Record<string, unknown> } }
interface ApprovalStep { kind: 'approval'; active: string; done: string; rejected: string; title: string; risk: Risk; lines: string[]; approveLabel: string; rejectLabel: string; editable?: { label: string; initial: string } }
interface OutputStep { kind: 'output'; active: string; done: string; output: string; outputIfRejected: string }
type RunStep = WorkStep | ApprovalStep | OutputStep;
interface RunScript { id: string; task: string; agent: string; requestedBy: string; model: string; steps: RunStep[] }
interface RunSummary { id: string; task: string; agent: string; outcome: 'waiting' | 'completed' | 'failed' | 'stopped' | 'rejected'; startedMinutesAgo: number; durationSec: number | null; reviewers: { name: string; initials: string }[] }
declare function scriptFor(runId: string): RunScript | undefined;
declare function listWaitingRuns(): RunSummary[];
declare function listRuns(signal: AbortSignal): Promise<RunSummary[]>;

Build the Assistant page of an admin console for a workspace of AI agents: a person asks questions about the workspace's agents and policies and gets answers. Each turn shows the question and then the answer, which streams in as it is written. Answers cite their sources inline, with the sources listed under the answer and openable. Each answer says how confident it is and why. An answer can come with a notice, a warning or a note, shown with it. Once an answer is complete it can be copied. The question box sends on Enter and stays usable on a phone. A long conversation shows how far down it the reader is. Required states: before anything is asked (with suggested questions), while an answer is being written, a question with no answer, and a failed answer that can be asked again.

The data layer exists. Copy these declarations into your file exactly as they are, and do not implement or import them:

type Segment = string | { cite: number };
interface Source { index: number; source: string; href?: string; passage: string }
interface CannedAnswer { id: string; question: string; segments: Segment[]; sources: Source[]; confidence: { level: 'low' | 'medium' | 'high'; basis: string } | null; notice?: { intent: 'warning' | 'information'; title: string; body: string } }
declare const SUGGESTED_QUESTIONS: string[];
declare function ask(question: string, signal: AbortSignal): AsyncIterable<{ answer: CannedAnswer; segmentsSoFar: number }>;

Build the Settings page of an admin console for a workspace of AI agents. Sections: Notifications (three switches: approval emails, failure alerts, weekly digest; each saves the moment it changes and says Saving, Saved or Not saved). Workspace defaults, saved together with one Save and a Discard: default model; log retention of 30, 90 or 365 days, with a short explanation of what retention means available beside it; redact personal data; require approval for new agents; the approvers in the order they are asked, reorderable, and at least one; quiet hours as a From and Until time each day; and the colour of the Approve button in approval emails, which must keep 4.5:1 contrast against its white text, refused as it changes and on save. API access: a CLI command and an example API request beside its response, each copyable. Danger zone: deleting the workspace, which asks for the password again. Required states: loading, a failed load, and a save that fails, keeping what was entered.

The data layer exists. Copy these declarations into your file exactly as they are, and do not implement or import them:

interface NotificationSettings { approvalEmails: boolean; failureAlerts: boolean; weeklyDigest: boolean }
interface WorkspaceDefaults { defaultModel: string; retentionDays: '30' | '90' | '365'; redactPii: boolean; approvalForNewAgents: boolean; approvers: string[]; quietFrom: string | null; quietUntil: string | null; emailAccent: string | null }
interface WorkspaceSettings { workspaceName: string; plan: 'Team' | 'Enterprise'; notifications: NotificationSettings; defaults: WorkspaceDefaults; deletionScheduledFor: string | null }
declare const MODELS: { value: string; label: string }[];
declare const MEMBERS: { id: string; name: string }[];
declare function getSettings(signal: AbortSignal): Promise<WorkspaceSettings>;
declare function saveNotification(key: keyof NotificationSettings, on: boolean): Promise<void>;
declare function saveDefaults(defaults: WorkspaceDefaults): Promise<{ ok: true } | { ok: false; message: string }>;
declare function scheduleDeletion(password: string): Promise<{ ok: boolean; message?: string }>;
